# Encapsulation

Encapsulation means:

> Bundle data and methods together.

Example:

```javascript
class BankAccount {

   constructor(balance) {
      this.balance = balance;
   }

   deposit(amount) {
      this.balance += amount;
   }

   withdraw(amount) {
      this.balance -= amount;
   }

}
```

Data:

```javascript
balance
```

Behavior:

```javascript
deposit()
withdraw()
```

inside same class.

---

# Example

```javascript
const account =
 new BankAccount(1000);

account.deposit(500);

console.log(
 account.balance
);
```

Output:

```text
1500
```

---

# Getters

Getter behaves like property.

Example:

```javascript
class User {

   constructor(name) {
      this.name = name;
   }

   get upperName() {
      return this.name.toUpperCase();
   }

}
```

Usage:

```javascript
const user =
 new User("Siddharth");

console.log(
 user.upperName
);
```

Output:

```text
SIDDHARTH
```

Notice:

```javascript
user.upperName
```

No parentheses.

---

# Setters

Setter updates value.

Example:

```javascript
class User {

   constructor(name) {
      this.name = name;
   }

   set updateName(newName) {
      this.name = newName;
   }

}
```

Usage:

```javascript
const user =
 new User("John");

user.updateName = "Alex";

console.log(user.name);
```

Output:

```text
Alex
```

---

# Static Methods

Static methods belong to class itself.

Not object.

Example:

```javascript
class MathUtils {

   static add(a,b) {
      return a+b;
   }

}
```

Usage:

```javascript
console.log(
 MathUtils.add(10,20)
);
```

Output:

```text
30
```

---

Wrong:

```javascript
const math =
 new MathUtils();

math.add();
```

Error.

Because:

```javascript
add()
```

is static.

---

